  "documentation": {
    "range": "This species<em>&#160;</em>occurs throughout much of Africa, with a range encompassing <span style=\"font-weight: bold;\">Mauritania</span>, <span style=\"font-weight: bold;\">Mali</span>, <span style=\"font-weight: bold;\">Niger</span>, <span style=\"font-weight: bold;\">Chad</span>, <span style=\"font-weight: bold;\">Sudan</span>, <span style=\"font-weight: bold;\">Ethiopia</span>, <span style=\"font-weight: bold;\">Eritrea</span>,&#160;<span style=\"font-weight: bold;\">Kenya</span>, <span style=\"font-weight: bold;\">Uganda</span>, <span style=\"font-weight: bold;\">Tanzania</span>, <span style=\"font-weight: bold;\">Angola</span>, <span style=\"font-weight: bold;\">Namibia</span>, <span style=\"font-weight: bold;\">South</span> <span style=\"font-weight: bold;\">Africa</span>, <span style=\"font-weight: bold;\">Botswana</span>, <span style=\"font-weight: bold;\">Zambia</span>, <span style=\"font-weight: bold;\">Zimbabwe </span>and <span style=\"font-weight: bold;\">Mozambique</span>. The subspecies <span style=\"font-style: italic;\">syriacus</span> of the Middle East is extinct, probably since around 1966 (del Hoyo <span style=\"font-style: italic;\">et al.</span> 1992).",
    "population": "Densities of 0.05-0.2 birds/km<sup>2</sup> have been reported (Brown <em>et al</em>. 1982) in short grass plains and semi-desert (i.e. most of the mapped range) and may exceptionally reach 0.6-0.8 birds/km<sup>2</sup> in some protected areas. This range is also congruent with densities reported by Okello <em>et al</em>. (2016) and Griffin <em>et al</em>. (2021) who, however, found much more variability in population density in protected areas (0.02-0.66). Across its entire mapped range, there is approximately 6,000,000 km<sup>2</sup> of suitable habitat (Jung <em>et al</em>. 2020). Applying a density of 0.05-0.2 to 6,000,000 km<sup>2</sup> yields a total estimate of 300,000-1,200,000. This value is broadly consistent with the estimate of 60,000 birds in Botswana (Moreki <em>et al.</em> 2021; which, however, lacks accompanying detail), which hosts approximately 10% of suitable habitat (Jung <em>et al</em>. 2020). A population size of 300,000-900,000 is therefore estimated.",
    "habitats": null,
    "threats": null,
    "measures": null,
    "use_trade": "Historically large harvest of the species for the plume trade caused declines. Domesticated and farmed widely. Commonly held in captivity for display. Hunting and egg collecting caused declines in the 20th century; continues but at a low rate.",
    "rationale": "This species has an extremely large range, and hence does not approach the thresholds for Vulnerable under the range size criterion (extent of occurrence &lt;20,000 km<sup>2</sup> combined with a declining or fluctuating range size, habitat extent/quality, or population size and a small number of locations or severe fragmentation). Despite the fact that the population trend appears to be decreasing, the decline is not believed to be sufficiently rapid to approach the thresholds for Vulnerable under the population trend criterion (&gt;30% decline over ten years or three generations). The population size is very large, and does not approach the thresholds for Vulnerable under the population size criterion (&lt;10,000 mature individuals with a continuing decline estimated to be &gt;10% in ten years or three generations, or with a specified population structure). For these reasons the species is evaluated as Least Concern.",
    "trend_justification": "This species has previously suffered owing to the plume trade and hunting, but is now most threatened by habitat loss (del Hoyo <span style=\"font-style: italic;\">et al.</span> 1992).",
    "taxonomic_notes": "<em>Struthio camelus</em> and <em>S. molybdophanes</em> (del Hoyo and Collar 2014) were previously lumped as <em>S. camelus</em> following Sibley and Monroe (1990, 1993)."
  },
